\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The default assumption of entity priority}

Since Aristotle established ``substance'' as the first category, Western physics has implicitly assumed a core presupposition: existence precedes relation. Entities are the bearers of attributes; relations are secondary connections between entities. Under this presupposition, diagnostic methods of physical systems also follow an entity-first logic: we measure magnetization (an entity attribute), compute energy gap (an entity excitation), and extract correlation functions (connections between entities). These coarse-grained entity diagnostics are presumed to be sufficient tools for capturing the physical behavior of a system.

\subsection{Independent dynamics of the relational field}

However, a different voice has persistently emerged from the depths of quantum many-body physics. The area law of entanglement entropy~\cite{Eisert2010} implies that the information content of a region is determined not by its volume (an entity attribute) but by the area of its boundary (a relational feature). The ground-state degeneracy of topological order is determined not by any local order parameter but by the global entanglement structure of the system~\cite{Wen2017}. Kitaev, in his description of Majorana zero modes, wrote: ``The ground state degeneracy is not a property of any individual electron, but of the entire system as a whole. The Majorana modes are non-local---they cannot be assigned to any single site. They exist in the correlations between sites''~\cite{Kitaev2001}.

These statements point in the same direction: {\em the relational field possesses dynamics that are independent of the entities.} A reorganization of the relational structure may occur in a manner that is completely invisible to coarse-grained entity diagnostics. This paper reports our systematic examination of this hypothesis.

\subsection{Collaborative mode and contribution structure}

The three authors of this paper used completely independent tools and methods to verify and diagnose the same set of public data:

\begin{itemize}
\item Guanghao Li (G.L.): designed and executed all DMRG and exact diagonalization experiments, constructed the relation-graph compiler, and proposed the operational definition and three laws of Silent Discordance.
\item Rastislav Draho\v{s}: using independent DMRG code, exact Bethe ansatz calculations, and boundary CFT theoretical analysis, independently verified every core discovery. He contributed four critical technical corrections (see Section~\ref{sec:drahos_contrib}).
