\section{Methodology: Principles and Constraints of Experimental Design}

\subsection{Five axioms as normative constraints}

The experimental design strictly follows five axiomatic constraints. Axiom~I (Relation Ontology) requires fixing the entity composition while varying only the relational configuration. Axiom~II (Contradiction Dynamics) requires implanting a contradiction edge in the relation graph to drive the evolution of the system. Axiom~III (Practical Intervention) requires simultaneously applying a default diagnosis and an enhanced diagnosis, and displaying their difference side by side. Axiom~IV (Resonance Tuning) requires not taking ``discovering new physics'' as the optimization target. Axiom~V (Meta-Reflection) requires honestly declaring the boundaries of the method and the limitations of the data.

\subsection{Rigorously preventing the tool-rationality paradox}

The concrete manifestation of the tool-rationality paradox in numerical research is: when a tool produces anomalous data, the researcher does not question the adequacy of the tool, but instead directly interprets the anomaly as a physical discovery~\cite{Li2026Relation}. This study adopts five preventive measures: predefining success criteria (fixed before experiment execution, not modified afterwards), uniform parameters, dual auditing, complete disclosure, and honest recording of negative results.

\subsection{Firmly opposing ``alignment''}

``Alignment'' in the context of this paper means retrospectively matching numerical results with theoretical expectations. In each experiment we predefine explicit success criteria before execution, and report them truthfully regardless of whether they are met.

\subsection{Depth reproducibility audit}

All valley depths reported in this paper were subjected to a reproducibility audit. For each representative topology, five independent runs were performed with distinct random Lanczos starting vectors, while keeping all physical parameters fixed. Two distinct reproducibility classes emerge:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Position robustness (class 0):} The valley position is exactly reproducible across all runs.
\item \textbf{Depth robustness (class 1):} The valley depth exhibits statistical scatter of approximately $6\%$--$20\%$ across runs. Depths are therefore reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation over five runs.
\end{itemize}

This distinction is essential for honest reporting: the valley position is the physically robust quantity, while the valley depth carries a numerical uncertainty that must be explicitly stated.

\section{Experiment I: First Discovery of Silent Discordance in the PXP Model}

\subsection{Experimental design}

We first tested the existence of Silent Discordance in the PXP model ($L=18$, constrained Hilbert space of about 6765 dimensions). The PXP model describes a one-dimensional Rydberg atom chain subject to the constraint that adjacent atoms cannot be simultaneously excited. A chemical potential defect $\mu$ is implanted at the central site and scanned from $0$ to $2.0$, while two diagnostics are applied simultaneously:

\begin{itemize}
\item Default diagnosis: mean of the absolute values of the spin correlation matrix (mean $|C|$).
\item Enhanced diagnosis: standard deviation of the half-chain (left 9 sites) entanglement spectrum.
\end{itemize}

Predefined success criterion: the enhanced diagnosis exhibits an abrupt change at some $\mu$ value, while the default diagnosis remains smooth at the same location.

